  69| ## 1 Introduction
 111| The objective is to find the feasible geometries for a 5 kW-class machine, and then characterise them.
 124| Loyd showed that both crosswind modes share one theoretical power ceiling [1].
 125| Diehl set the classification axes [2].
 126| Trevisi built one power model for both modes and found the glide-ratio flip [3].
 127| Cherubini mapped the ground-gen and fly-gen taxonomy [4].
 128| Van der Burg found no dominant wing design [5].
 131| Van de Kaa and Kamp found no dominant generation architecture [6].
 132| Pereira ranked the design factors and found that many small units beat few large ones [7].
 134| Flather recorded the rope-drive physics that the TRPT descends from [8].
 135| Benhaïem and Schmehl analysed the torque path of a rotating reel [9].
 136| Tulloch modelled the Daisy TRPT and checked the model against measured flight windows [10].
 137| Daisy is the experimental prototype rotary kite turbine whose measured flight data anchor the model (NR-056).
 140| ## 2 Methods
 142| ### 2.1 The kite turbine and its terms
 176| The power sizing sets the rotor radius from the power, the wind speed and the power coefficient (NR-050).
 178| The base span is 0.75 of the power-sized rotor radius, and the base chord is 0.113 of it.
 185| Its tip sits at 5.630 m from the shaft axis (NR-032).
 187| The power sizing inverts the power equation (NR-050):
 189| $$r_{\mathrm{rotor}}=\sqrt{\frac{P}{f_{\mathrm{proj}}\,C_{p}\,0.5\,\rho\,\pi\,v^{3}}}$$
 194| The winner decode gives r_rotor 2.434 m (NR-050).
 198| The shaft transition cone holds one fixed half-angle, 22 degrees, by construction.
 200| It is not a search value (NR-049).
 202| That 22 measures one side of the cone, from the shaft axis out to the cone wall.
 203| A reference machine prints the same 22 as the angle across the full apex, one side against the other.
 220| [Figure seated: figure-map row 2 (system anatomy plate, slug `system-anatomy`). Values cite NR-011, NR-017 and NR-043.]
 222| ### 2.2 The simulation analysis pipeline instrument and its terms
 224| #### 2.2.1 The gene
 233| The winner genome names its genes in the decode fields (NR-011).
 235| The genome carries ten slots, x1 to x10.
 240| | x1 | hub ring radius r_hub | 0.700 to 7.776 m |
 241| | x2 | ground ring radius r_bottom | 0.398 to 1.195 m |
 242| | x3 | common length-to-radius target L/r | 1.0 to 2.94 |
 243| | x4 | polygon side count n_lines | 3 to 9 |
 244| | x5 | ring density bias | -0.8 to 0.8 |
 245| | x6 | rotor count | 1 to 3 |
 246| | x7 | bank at the topmost rotor | 0 to 22 deg |
 247| | x8 | bank at the lowest rotor | 0 to 22 deg |
 248| | x9 | blade scale at the topmost rotor | 0.2 to 1.0 |
 249| | x10 | blade scale at the lowest rotor | 0.2 to 1.0 |
 251| The decoder reads x6 as a rotor count.
 255| The winner carries one active rotor, so x8 and x10 stay unused (NR-011).
 256| The decoder also rounds x4, the polygon side count, to an integer (NR-011).
 261| The winner reads six rings (NR-011).
 263| #### 2.2.2 The evaluator
 270| That state comes from a simplified power balance (NR-025).
 271| The relax run takes 10 seconds.
 273| The measurement window takes 40 seconds.
 276| The evaluator scores the power over the measurement window only (NR-015).
 281| P_score reads the tail-5 power, the mean of the last five seconds of the window (NR-046, NR-041).
 283| The twist ratio is the absolute segment twist divided by the crossing limit, the worst segment over the window (NR-046).
 284| The crossing limit follows (NR-053):
 286| $$\delta\alpha^{*}=2\arcsin\left(\frac{L_{\mathrm{seg}}}{\sqrt{2\left(L_{\mathrm{seg}}^{2}+2r_{\mathrm{seg}}^{2}\right)}}\right)$$
